# ── Byte-exact artifacts ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are COMMITTED ARTIFACTS (PRD §R2.3, §R2.4, §G6). `msat ceiling --verify`
# regenerates each one and compares it BYTE FOR BYTE against the committed file,
# then exits non-zero on any difference. That is the right test, and it is
# unpassable without this file.
#
# With no `.gitattributes`, git falls back to `core.autocrlf`. On Windows that
# is `true` by default, so checkout rewrites LF -> CRLF and the working-tree
# bytes stop matching the committed bytes. Measured on 2026-10-14, all three
# artifacts, with identical content and zero real drift:
#
#     HEAD blob (LF)          14,483 B
#     working tree (CRLF)     15,050 B   <- what --verify reads
#     regenerated (LF)        14,466 B   <- what --verify compares against
#
# The 584-byte gap is the newline count, nothing else. `msat ceiling --verify`
# therefore reports DRIFT at byte 1 on every artifact, forever, on Windows - and
# a DRIFT report that is always red is worse than no report, because the next
# real drift is invisible inside the noise.
#
# `-text` marks these as binary: git performs no conversion in either direction,
# so the working tree holds exactly the committed bytes on every platform.
# This is the right fix at the source rather than normalising inside the
# comparator, which would hide the conversion instead of preventing it.
msat/results/*.json -text
msat/data.lock         -text

# Everything else keeps normal handling; only byte-compared artifacts are pinned.